This is part six of [A History of Coffee](/coffee/guides/coffee-history/). It steps back from Britain ([part five](/coffee/guides/coffee-history-turn-to-tea/)) to look at how coffee went from one corner of Arabia to the whole tropical world. It also explains why so many of the varieties and origins you see on bags today are where they are.

It's not all a happy story. Most of the coffee drunk in Europe and America in the 1700s and 1800s was grown by enslaved or forced labor.

## Breaking Yemen's monopoly

For about two centuries, nearly all coffee came from Yemen ([part two](/coffee/guides/coffee-history-islamic-world/)). That ended with a few smuggled seeds and plants.

- **India, 1600s:** The story goes that a Sufi pilgrim named **Baba Budan** smuggled seven coffee seeds out of Yemen, strapped to his chest, and planted them in the hills of Chikmagalur in southern India. The details are legend, but coffee did take root there, and the area still grows it.
- **The Dutch, 1616 to 1690s:** A Dutch merchant, Pieter van den Broecke, took coffee plants from Mocha to Amsterdam in 1616. The Dutch East India Company later planted coffee in its colonies, first in India and Ceylon, then on **Java**, where plantations were established by 1699. "Java" became slang for coffee.

The Dutch did very well from this. Their colonial government later forced Javanese farmers to grow coffee and other export crops for the state (the "Cultivation System," from 1830). A Dutch novel about the abuses, _Max Havelaar_ (1860), caused a scandal, and more than a century later, the first Fairtrade label was named after it.

## One plant in Paris

In 1706, the Dutch brought a coffee plant from Java to the botanical garden in Amsterdam. In **1714**, the city gave a descendant of that plant to **Louis XIV** of France, who had it grown in a greenhouse in Paris.

That single plant, or plants like it, became the ancestor of the **Typica** variety, and of a huge share of the coffee grown in the Americas. It's a big reason why coffee from the Americas started with so little genetic variety compared to Ethiopia (see [Coffee Varieties](/coffee/guides/coffee-varieties/)).

The French also planted coffee from Yemen on the island of Bourbon (now Réunion) in the Indian Ocean, starting in 1715. The plants that grew there became the **Bourbon** variety, which is still one of the best-loved in specialty coffee.

## Martinique and the Caribbean

Around **1723**, a French naval officer, **Gabriel de Clieu**, took a seedling from Paris to the French colony of **Martinique**. He later wrote that he shared his own water ration with it during a long voyage. It's hard to know how much of that is embellished, but coffee did spread from Martinique across the Caribbean.

The French colony of **Saint-Domingue** (now Haiti) became the world's biggest coffee producer. By the late 1780s it grew around half the world's coffee, on plantations worked by hundreds of thousands of enslaved Africans under brutal conditions. In **1791**, the enslaved population rose up in the Haitian Revolution, which eventually created the first independent Black republic in 1804. Haiti's coffee exports collapsed, and the market shifted elsewhere.

## Brazil

In **1727**, a Portuguese officer, **Francisco de Melo Palheta**, brought coffee to **Brazil** from French Guiana. The legend says the governor's wife slipped him seeds hidden in a bouquet of flowers.

Brazil grew slowly at first, then exploded in the 1800s. By the mid-1800s, it was the **largest coffee producer in the world**, and it still is. That growth was built on slavery: Brazil imported more enslaved Africans than any other country, and many were sent to coffee farms. Brazil didn't abolish slavery until **1888**, the last country in the Americas to do so. After that, millions of European immigrants, many from Italy, came to work the coffee farms.

## Central and South America

Through the 1800s, coffee spread across Latin America:

- **Costa Rica** was one of the first, growing coffee early in the 1800s and shipping it to England by the 1840s. Coffee money built much of the country.
- **Guatemala and El Salvador** turned to coffee in the second half of the 1800s. Governments seized communal land from Indigenous communities and passed laws forcing people to work on plantations.
- **Colombia** grew into a major producer in the late 1800s and early 1900s, mostly on small family farms, which is still the pattern there today.
- **Mexico** and the rest of Central America followed.

These are the same countries in the [Coffee Origins](/coffee/guides/coffee-origins/) chart, and the varieties planted then, Typica and Bourbon, are the parents of many varieties grown there now.

## America picks coffee

In the American colonies, tea and coffee were both popular until the **Boston Tea Party** in 1773, when colonists dumped East India Company tea into Boston Harbor to protest British taxes. Afterward, drinking tea started to look unpatriotic. John Adams wrote to his wife that tea "must be universally renounced."

Coffee was also closer and cheaper, coming from the Caribbean and later Brazil. By the 1800s, the US was one of the world's biggest coffee drinkers.

- **Hawaii, 1828:** A missionary planted coffee in Kona, from Brazilian plants that had arrived on Oʻahu a few years earlier. Kona is now one of the few places in the US that grows coffee.
- **The Civil War:** Union soldiers got coffee as part of their rations and drank enormous amounts of it. A Smithsonian curator who read through their diaries found that the word "coffee" came up more often than "war," "bullet" or "Lincoln."
- **Roasted coffee for sale:** Most people roasted green beans at home in a pan. In 1868, **John Arbuckle** patented a glaze of egg and sugar that kept roasted beans fresh, and his brand, Arbuckles' Ariosa, sold pre-roasted coffee in one-pound bags. It became a staple of the American West.
- **Brands:** **Folgers** started in San Francisco during the 1850 Gold Rush, and **Maxwell House** was named after a hotel in Nashville in 1892. These were the start of the big canned brands of the next century.

## The fungus that made Ceylon a tea country

In **1869**, a fungus called **coffee leaf rust** showed up on coffee plantations in **Ceylon** (now Sri Lanka), which was then one of the world's biggest growers. It spreads on the wind, covers the leaves with orange spots and weakens the plant until it stops producing.

Within about 20 years it had wiped out Ceylon's coffee industry. Planters ripped out the coffee and planted tea instead, which is how Ceylon became famous for tea (see [part five](/coffee/guides/coffee-history-turn-to-tea/)). The rust spread through Asia and Africa, and it's still the biggest disease threat to coffee today.

It also led to the spread of **robusta** (_Coffea canephora_), a different species found growing wild in central Africa in the late 1800s. It's tougher and resists rust better, though it tastes harsher. Today it makes up a big share of the world's coffee, much of it in instant coffee and espresso blends.

## By 1900

Coffee had gone from one country to dozens, across Asia, Africa and the Americas. Brazil dominated, prices swung wildly, and the stage was set for the 20th century: espresso, instant coffee, the coffee can, and eventually the specialty coffee you can buy today.

